# Fade in swapped content

When a toggle, switch, tab or similar control replaces or reveals a block of content, fade the new content in so the change isn't jarring. Use the existing `animate-fade-in-fast` (0.2s) class, or `animate-fade-in` for larger, slower reveals.

Content that is already mounted doesn't replay a CSS animation, so give the swapped block a `key` that changes with the control's value; it remounts and fades in each time.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — the essay snaps between versions
{source === "mine" ? <AiEssay /> : <PublishedEssay />}

// ✅ GOOD — keyed wrapper remounts and fades in on every switch
<div key={source} className="animate-fade-in-fast">
  {source === "mine" ? <AiEssay /> : <PublishedEssay />}
</div>
```

Keep it to a short opacity fade; no slides or bounces for plain content swaps.
